#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208276 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d08beac-6261-53bb-a993-ae50e3e4e577" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation"}] :question/id #uuid "84213a67-69f3-5f2a-a3b0-f0309257e545" :question/math-academy-id "e-18338" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ with probabilities $0.2,$ $0.5,$ and $0.3,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y=u] = 6, \\quad \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y=v] = 9, \\quad \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y=w] = 3.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\textrm{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum_y \\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = y] \\cdot P(Y = y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y = u$ with probability $0.2,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = u] = 6$\n\n- $Y = v$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = v] = 9$\n\n- $Y = w$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = w] = 3$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=u]\\cdot P(Y=u)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=v]\\cdot P(Y=v) \\\\  & \\,+\\text{E}[X∣Y=w]\\cdot P(Y=w) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.2+9\\cdot 0.5+3\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =1.2+4.5+0.9 \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208277 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680dff97-3a2b-57ad-b234-c6184b9ef867" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "0a8fcaef-ae9f-5e55-acb1-58a34cfa2894" :question/math-academy-id "e-18339" :question/problem "A wildlife researcher is sent to either Forest $1$ or Forest $2$ to record animal sightings. With probability $0.7,$ he is sent to Forest $1,$ where the expected number of sightings in a day is $6.2.$ With probability $0.3,$ he is sent to Forest $2,$ where the expected number is $3.8.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of animal sightings in a day?" :question/worked-solution "We are asked to compute the expected number of animal sightings the researcher records daily. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the forest where the researcher is stationed.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of animal sightings in a day, and let $Y$ be the forest to which the researcher is assigned.\n\n- $Y = 1$ with probability $0.7,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = 1] = 6.2.$\n\n- $Y = 2$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = 2] = 3.8.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]\\cdot P(Y=2) \\\\  & =6.2\\cdot 0.7+3.8\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =4.34+1.14 \\\\  & =5.48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208278 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208562 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca941236-4ae9-5740-bfbf-d15eb758212a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property"}] :question/id #uuid "4ccdabba-d496-575e-99d6-ad102aca2811" :question/math-academy-id "e-18340" :question/problem "Let $Y$ be a discrete random variable, and suppose $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y] = 3Y + 2.$ If $\\textrm{E}[X] = 20,$ what is $\\textrm{E}[Y]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given an expression for $\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and the value of $\\textrm{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation, which gives\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[3Y + 2].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[3Y+2] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 20,$ so we solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =3\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+2 \\\\ 18 & =3\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208279 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208563 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "753c2aae-9fc7-5b5b-930e-87222f30f7a2" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "f6e9b398-63d8-5e0a-8333-7d8971e6d261" :question/math-academy-id "e-18341" :question/problem "In a fantasy card game, a random mana level $m$ is drawn uniformly from $1$ to $201.$ A player selects a random spell costing an integer amount of mana between $1$ and $m,$ chosen uniformly at random. What is the expected cost of the spell selected?" :question/worked-solution "This is a two-step random process: first, a mana level $m$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $201,$ and then a spell is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $m.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the mana cost selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number, the mana level drawn uniformly from $1$ to $201.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the cost of the spell selected, uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\textrm{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y = m,$ the value of $X$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1, 2, \\dots, m\\},$ so\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y = m] = \\frac{m + 1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}\\left[\\frac{1 + Y}{2}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[1+Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1, 2, \\dots, 201\\},$ we compute\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\frac{1 + 201}{2} = 101.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+101) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =51.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d08beac-6261-53bb-a993-ae50e3e4e577" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1528688-5feb-5078-98bb-a2e04a2ac756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205397} {:answer/id #uuid "fd941727-2fb5-5c25-9731-f4e83b2213c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205398} {:answer/id #uuid "198ef7a7-2bb0-59d8-b373-113d4a2fbb6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205399} {:answer/id #uuid "6138216f-08a9-57ea-90dd-eab1c27d685f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205400} {:answer/id #uuid "67621105-a3ef-54a4-a4aa-23a746c189b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6138216f-08a9-57ea-90dd-eab1c27d685f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205400} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9a5d481-7af7-5a7b-bb08-bb6c306770d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6259685-7284-5ef0-b466-01829221574a" :question/math-academy-id "q-275328" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $1$ and $2$ with probabilities $0.3$ and $0.7,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=5,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]=8\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are asked to compute the expected value of $X,$ given conditional expectations of $X$ based on the value of another random variable $Y.$ This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=5$\n\n- $Y=2$ with probability $0.7,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]=8$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]\\cdot P(Y=2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.3+8\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =1.5+5.6 \\\\  & =7.1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d08beac-6261-53bb-a993-ae50e3e4e577" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd1c2f73-46d9-5192-b6fe-b5eec80d766b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205403} {:answer/id #uuid "f47658ca-fb4b-5f5c-a692-9ef41918fb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205404} {:answer/id #uuid "11f24875-c5fa-5c8f-9a06-cad95b1cff30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205405} {:answer/id #uuid "25ee348d-cd16-5051-bf50-8a7eb9121006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205406} {:answer/id #uuid "739dabea-2fea-56f9-ae8b-ad1beb0ad120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "739dabea-2fea-56f9-ae8b-ad1beb0ad120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205407} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfed4602-9f65-5da2-a9c7-07675109962a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f13e8cc-869b-5c86-ae8a-0a5ace2cea2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-275329" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ with probabilities $0.25,$ $0.5,$ and $0.25,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=10,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=c]=6.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=a$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=10$\n\n- $Y=b$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4$\n\n- $Y=c$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=c]=6$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]\\cdot P(Y=a)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]\\cdot P(Y=b) \\\\  & \\,+\\text{E}[X∣Y=c]\\cdot P(Y=c) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.5+6\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5+2+1.5 \\\\  & =6.0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680dff97-3a2b-57ad-b234-c6184b9ef867" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad36734-d9b3-5129-9113-dc04e511be28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad36734-d9b3-5129-9113-dc04e511be28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530883} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8515f8b-2618-5ee6-abbc-c44663bca517" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5a708e9-c589-5fc2-b4d6-ac87be196ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-275331" :question/problem "A surveyor is sent to one of three cities: $A,$ $B,$ or $C.$ She is sent to City $A$ with a probability of $0.4,$ City $B$ with a probability of $0.35,$ and City $C$ with a probability of $0.25.$  \n\nIf the expected number of households visited is $12$ in City $A,$ $18$ in City $B,$ and $10$ in City $C,$ what is the expected number of households visited on a given day?\n\nThe expected number of households visited is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We are given the probabilities of being assigned to one of three cities and the expected number of households visited in each. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which tells us how to compute the overall expected value by conditioning on which city the surveyor is sent to.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of households visited in a day, and let $Y$ be the city to which the surveyor is sent.\n\n- $Y=A$ with probability $0.4,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]=12.$\n\n- $Y=B$ with probability $0.35,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]=18.$\n\n- $Y=C$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=C]=10.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]\\cdot P(Y=A)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]\\cdot P(Y=B) \\\\  & \\,+\\text{E}[X∣Y=C]\\cdot P(Y=C) \\\\  & =12\\cdot 0.4+18\\cdot 0.35+10\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =4.8+6.3+2.5 \\\\  & =13.6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208562 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca941236-4ae9-5740-bfbf-d15eb758212a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd6dc757-233a-599b-bd5c-c9c1f5671465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205416} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec876ef-0842-551a-a7c4-183d63812195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205417} {:answer/id #uuid "17c18b75-7be5-5753-a8d5-ef94d2555ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205418} {:answer/id #uuid "25673941-b665-51ae-8b20-fffc28c862aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205419} {:answer/id #uuid "40fa31aa-f5dd-5db5-b73f-e662b6bdf2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17c18b75-7be5-5753-a8d5-ef94d2555ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205418} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d8db12-6740-5f7a-8680-be66f91aa038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a526eb82-61a0-550d-a495-93a0477c8895" :question/math-academy-id "q-275332" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=2Y+1.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=3,$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[2Y+1]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[2Y+1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208563 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "753c2aae-9fc7-5b5b-930e-87222f30f7a2" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e2416bc-10f6-56f3-bd9a-700211f58288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205422} {:answer/id #uuid "076776e5-b667-59a4-84b9-1e52b6dd8ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205423} {:answer/id #uuid "22888877-196b-5ad9-90d0-33e6be3ecef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205424} {:answer/id #uuid "28079649-5088-53cc-aee6-042af1b8e1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205425} {:answer/id #uuid "38619f94-a7b1-5343-9432-8fb2e1bb7228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28079649-5088-53cc-aee6-042af1b8e1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205425} :answer-field/id #uuid "f113a59f-96d4-5fb6-8b05-7aca73f2282c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4161b21b-2875-5896-b6b1-cbb123bca0ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-275334" :question/problem "A game randomly selects an integer $n$ from $1$ to $301,$ with each value equally likely. Then, a player is asked to guess an integer uniformly and randomly from $1$ to $n.$ What is the expected value of the player’s guess?" :question/worked-solution "This is a two-step random process: first, an integer $n$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $301,$ and then a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the number selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $301.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the player’s guess, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{1+Y}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[1+Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,301\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1+301}{2}=151.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+151) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 152 \\\\  & =76.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208563 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "753c2aae-9fc7-5b5b-930e-87222f30f7a2" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "630c2241-afa1-5bd5-9578-2f06d9c7212c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "630c2241-afa1-5bd5-9578-2f06d9c7212c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531080} :answer-field/id #uuid "43b17d3b-03b0-5905-9862-543ee5a6fde9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8f9a785-f3a0-5c7d-87e9-2dbe1e634d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-275335" :question/problem "A video game randomly selects a level difficulty $d$ from $1$ to $500,$ with each value equally likely. Once the difficulty is chosen, a treasure chest is placed in one of $d$ zones within the level, numbered from $1$ to $d,$ with each zone equally likely to receive the chest.\n\nWhat is the expected number of the zone where the chest appears?\n\nThe expected number of the zone where the chest appears is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "This is a two-step random process: first, a level difficulty is selected uniformly from the integers $1$ through $500,$ and then a treasure chest is placed uniformly in one of the zones numbered $1$ through $d,$ where $d$ is the selected difficulty.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the difficulty level of the chosen level. Since the difficulty is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $500,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,500\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the number of the zone where the chest is placed, which is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=d,$ the zone is selected uniformly from $1$ to $d.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=d]=\\frac{d+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,500\\},$ we compute\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1+500}{2}=250.5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (250.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 251.5 \\\\  & =125.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208562 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca941236-4ae9-5740-bfbf-d15eb758212a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bce4a05-5700-536d-aac0-b073f48479b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205429} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3a2ab4-c2d1-5222-b4db-20fd84eca4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205430} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f6d382-c842-5005-ad09-bb5c9c65712a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205431} {:answer/id #uuid "e34f8821-9f79-5474-8ecd-5d92a48de232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205432} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fe3502-3bb0-54cd-852b-42447ee6aaf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bce4a05-5700-536d-aac0-b073f48479b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205429} :answer-field/id #uuid "89e13b95-704a-58c4-858a-205f7196eeb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0530ea25-cf6d-5be9-b889-2267d0dcbc09" :question/math-academy-id "q-275341" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=5Y-4.$ If $\\text{E}[X]=11,$ what is $\\text{E}[Y]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and the value of $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation, which gives\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[5Y-4].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[5Y-4] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\text{E}[X]=11,$ so we solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =5\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-4 \\\\ 15 & =5\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d08beac-6261-53bb-a993-ae50e3e4e577" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35aec184-a511-5be8-a507-0771ade9da57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530978} {:answer/id #uuid "5705a760-886d-5f55-af83-b5258fc3c589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531056} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7e73c9-fbbc-51f1-8e60-c8d5c4145021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531104} {:answer/id #uuid "c867db21-96a9-5ec9-ad70-6ff7ad8bac73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531282} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ce6be2-246b-5b58-97cc-84990fd5de70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b7e73c9-fbbc-51f1-8e60-c8d5c4145021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531104} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8474380-6762-5e2b-a828-de08bc8f4b07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb48c25d-7d3e-5c68-83ac-d6034fcc4a05" :question/math-academy-id "q-275424" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $a$ and $b$ with probabilities $0.55$ and $0.45,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=6,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are asked to compute the expected value of $X,$ given conditional expectations of $X$ based on the value of another random variable $Y.$ This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y=a$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=6$\n\n- $Y=b$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]\\cdot P(Y=a)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]\\cdot P(Y=b) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.55+4\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =3.3+1.8 \\\\  & =5.1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d08beac-6261-53bb-a993-ae50e3e4e577" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f4c462-5661-5976-aa00-f293611bd8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530865} {:answer/id #uuid "360c385c-7cb0-5d64-98b2-ff81a8374aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530981} {:answer/id #uuid "63f05175-1e59-535e-9b01-7f59bc3c90e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531083} {:answer/id #uuid "9521cc63-f41b-5956-a47b-e64eb1aeaba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531186} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b854fb-2d2f-5896-abed-cca0f157da5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01f4c462-5661-5976-aa00-f293611bd8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530865} :answer-field/id #uuid "96bbda27-3868-52f3-95a1-2c04686d229f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d436b5a-22ca-50fe-bbca-e3c952622b7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-275428" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $p,$ $q,$ and $r$ with probabilities $0.5,$ $0.3,$ and $0.2,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]=8,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]=14,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]=10.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=p$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]=8$\n\n- $Y=q$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]=14$\n\n- $Y=r$ with probability $0.2,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]=10$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]\\cdot P(Y=p)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]\\cdot P(Y=q) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]\\cdot P(Y=r) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.5+14\\cdot 0.3+10\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4+4.2+2 \\\\  & =10.2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d08beac-6261-53bb-a993-ae50e3e4e577" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc16bed-211b-571b-9c24-c04e7bd06319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530969} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3312c2-ce2d-59c7-b9f4-a171218a11b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531024} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5a3570-3cf0-55e5-b174-12b466051ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531109} {:answer/id #uuid "75dd2ec8-ff16-5656-926a-2f44a28bf8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531125} {:answer/id #uuid "c93028d8-49e0-50fa-b211-b09c7b666a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d5a3570-3cf0-55e5-b174-12b466051ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531109} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d65bc33-87c9-5ab8-9039-3010d73546b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3d68253-5058-5406-8e0e-23e4aaba826a" :question/math-academy-id "q-275432" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $-1,$ $0,$ and $1$ with probabilities $0.15,$ $0.55,$ and $0.3,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]=4,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]=9,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=8.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=-1$ with probability $0.15,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]=4$\n\n- $Y=0$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]=9$\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=8$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]\\cdot P(Y=-1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.15+9\\cdot 0.55+8\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6+4.95+2.4 \\\\  & =7.95\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680dff97-3a2b-57ad-b234-c6184b9ef867" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3985986c-c90d-5143-8b6a-1649e262e690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530988} {:answer/id #uuid "44301662-2ef4-5c3f-8e9b-ab1b90a50a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531008} {:answer/id #uuid "5168d2e7-290a-5266-ac21-33841c67db65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531042} {:answer/id #uuid "da687fdf-6465-5cd3-844f-d6a2b6e8f010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531310} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1c5b93-cc04-5959-8a00-8c6d5ed8a3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5168d2e7-290a-5266-ac21-33841c67db65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531042} :answer-field/id #uuid "7107c288-407f-5c22-be30-7bf088a0a757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0fafa34-8718-5c91-847f-90668c3f437e" :question/math-academy-id "q-275440" :question/problem "A tutoring agency assigns a tutor to either High School $A$ or High School $B.$ With probability $0.45,$ the tutor is sent to High School $A,$ where the expected number of students tutored in a day is $4.8.$ With probability $0.55,$ the tutor is sent to High School $B,$ where the expected number of students tutored is $6.2.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of students tutored in a day?" :question/worked-solution "We are asked to compute the expected number of students tutored per day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the high school the tutor is assigned to.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of students tutored in a day, and let $Y$ be the school to which the tutor is assigned.\n\n- $Y=A$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]=4.8.$\n\n- $Y=B$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]=6.2.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]\\cdot P(Y=A)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]\\cdot P(Y=B) \\\\  & =4.8\\cdot 0.45+6.2\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =2.16+3.41 \\\\  & =5.57\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680dff97-3a2b-57ad-b234-c6184b9ef867" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2339995c-293a-5e38-b75f-583b94bb3fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "114.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530941} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcf6b66-bdce-5940-b915-81dcdd0dd19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "111.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530992} {:answer/id #uuid "8928a195-7188-5e31-9b90-cb91da301cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531165} {:answer/id #uuid "9833c4d9-c22a-5a16-a11a-7bcabe4a936e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531190} {:answer/id #uuid "e064cd7c-03b0-527c-8e07-df8848c5ab25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bcf6b66-bdce-5940-b915-81dcdd0dd19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "111.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530992} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a6fd0d-84d2-5683-803a-b9493ca6e2c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87aec959-895e-5be8-8c69-b3c4045c5829" :question/math-academy-id "q-275442" :question/problem "An order picker in a warehouse is assigned to either Section $1$ or Section $2$ on any given day. With probability $0.65,$ the order picker works in Section $1,$ where the expected number of orders picked per day is $120.$ With probability $0.35,$ the order picker works in Section $2,$ where the expected number of orders picked per day is $95.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of orders picked per day?" :question/worked-solution "We are asked to compute the expected number of orders picked per day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the section to which the picker is assigned.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of orders picked, and let $Y$ be the warehouse section to which the picker is assigned.\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.65,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=120.$\n\n- $Y=2$ with probability $0.35,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]=95.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]\\cdot P(Y=2) \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.65+95\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =78+33.25 \\\\  & =111.25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680dff97-3a2b-57ad-b234-c6184b9ef867" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebfc5d8-7755-54b9-912e-c479df8e0d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530923} {:answer/id #uuid "4e540e23-280f-5784-b5b7-b1fed19afe8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531031} {:answer/id #uuid "a45626ac-9eeb-5cb2-94e4-c2afebfdf1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531219} {:answer/id #uuid "ec127202-7f79-5f14-be98-442abd6566f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531338} {:answer/id #uuid "efd06613-add9-5cb6-9c13-d6a82ba9bff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e540e23-280f-5784-b5b7-b1fed19afe8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531031} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa1202d-fa18-55bd-a2d1-4f1dd6925f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e734511f-0834-5433-966a-4ae3851cf360" :question/math-academy-id "q-275443" :question/problem "A ride-share driver starts every day in either the City Center or the Suburban Area. The driver starts the day in the City Center with probability $0.55,$ where the expected number of rides given per day is $14.3.$ The driver starts the day in the Suburban Area with probability $0.45,$ where the expected number of rides is $11.7.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of rides given in a day?" :question/worked-solution "We are asked to compute the expected number of rides given in a day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the driver's starting location.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of rides completed in a day, and $Y$ be the location from which the driver started.\n\n- $Y=\\text{City}$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{City}]=14.3.$\n\n- $Y=\\text{Suburban}$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{Suburban}]=11.7.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{City}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{City}) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{Suburban}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{Suburban}) \\\\  & =14.3\\cdot 0.55+11.7\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =7.865+5.265 \\\\  & =13.13\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680dff97-3a2b-57ad-b234-c6184b9ef867" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expectation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7bd3040-6ef9-5072-98f9-d1080e7ac6c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7bd3040-6ef9-5072-98f9-d1080e7ac6c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531225} :answer-field/id #uuid "024c3696-4bfc-53f3-92aa-3d9e2a4aac8f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdbfe9c8-c3f2-5a6b-9ce7-99052b0abd68" :question/math-academy-id "q-275520" :question/problem "A salesperson visits one of three regions: North, South, or East. The probabilities that they are sent North, South, and East are $0.25,$ $0.5,$ and $0.25$ respectively.\n\nIf the expected number of sales made daily is $9$ in the North, $14$ in the South, and $11$ in the East, what is the expected number of sales made in a day?\n\nThe expected number of sales made is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We are given the probability of being sent to one of three regions and the expected number of sales made. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which tells us how to compute the overall expected value by conditioning on which region the salesperson visits.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sales made in a day, and let $Y$ be the region the salesperson visited.\n\n- $Y=\\text{North}$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{North}]=9.$\n\n- $Y=\\text{South}$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{South}]=14.$\n\n- $Y=\\text{East}$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{East}]=11.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{North}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{North})+\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{South}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{South}) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{East}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{East}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 0.25+14\\cdot 0.5+11\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.25+7+2.75 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208562 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca941236-4ae9-5740-bfbf-d15eb758212a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03860298-0f63-5504-87bf-85c028b3a592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530868} {:answer/id #uuid "4e61cd89-a242-5216-a20c-e05a7e080c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531033} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5ece32-b4ba-5674-8e4e-857a5d3de455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531203} {:answer/id #uuid "deb8feb4-d70c-584b-b26a-23713cd34220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531319} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc111df-6c57-5ca0-ae26-ce4da622b5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e61cd89-a242-5216-a20c-e05a7e080c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531033} :answer-field/id #uuid "eddd8d9a-b8c8-5b64-931c-553d29bea668" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eff3dc19-9675-582f-bb2d-ae75355788e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-275531" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=4Y-3.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=10,$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[4Y-3]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[4Y-3] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[3] \\\\  & =4\\cdot 10-3 \\\\  & =37\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208562 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca941236-4ae9-5740-bfbf-d15eb758212a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081b8e1b-1cb3-543b-9409-ebd64f7828d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530876} {:answer/id #uuid "1fce9e9b-9b99-550d-b6b3-9f794430e98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530927} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd9549c-5e7d-5f20-acda-21283ef2b8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531170} {:answer/id #uuid "a83bbdf8-1ade-579f-bb74-f6b17ea4146a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531227} {:answer/id #uuid "ea76e181-16a3-5d50-9003-e112be2d1b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a83bbdf8-1ade-579f-bb74-f6b17ea4146a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531227} :answer-field/id #uuid "105ca86f-b24b-5982-836e-67345b1718da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fde0d020-b26d-5edb-a043-de5b1f35d4a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-275538" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=\\sqrt{3},$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208562 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca941236-4ae9-5740-bfbf-d15eb758212a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3abc4c6c-e992-5e50-8cbc-4cecc9be41da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530989} {:answer/id #uuid "42094129-8f02-5060-8d21-acf753bc6258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531004} {:answer/id #uuid "491ce60c-f9f3-5ec1-877e-b9b0f2a1a805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531020} {:answer/id #uuid "52a4c2bf-387d-5b7e-bf21-ae9983d935b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531046} {:answer/id #uuid "e8375514-a11d-5124-8eae-6634cb095e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42094129-8f02-5060-8d21-acf753bc6258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531004} :answer-field/id #uuid "54959153-de5a-5466-a4b1-49e8eaebfe70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a2b9353-2f5e-55ce-b33e-819639da3c0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-275544" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=2Y-2.$ If $\\text{E}[X]=8,$ what is $\\text{E}[Y]?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and the value of $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation, which gives\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[2Y-2].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[2Y-2] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\text{E}[X]=8,$ so we solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-2 \\\\ 10 & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208563 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "753c2aae-9fc7-5b5b-930e-87222f30f7a2" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f40d701-fe11-5693-bf29-41be6a09d080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530924} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2f3be2-1044-5aba-ace8-2bb9c009ba74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531035} {:answer/id #uuid "87dcfc6f-0edf-5f39-92e6-578b03e8ce51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531161} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1fadd1-916b-5457-88b4-0b493db17e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531196} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3f8d2a-831e-5d39-947f-044c27f1b143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.00" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f2f3be2-1044-5aba-ace8-2bb9c009ba74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531035} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03c0e02-f315-5d2a-8a1e-94db11dbd1fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12dc9838-1006-5e99-98b8-8a2304014d14" :question/math-academy-id "q-275576" :question/problem "In a puzzle game, a room's difficulty level $n$ is selected randomly from $1$ to $120,$ with each value equally likely. Inside each room are $n$ puzzle pieces numbered from $1$ to $n,$ and one of them is selected uniformly at random. What is the expected value of the number on the selected puzzle piece?" :question/worked-solution "This is a two-step random process: first, a room of difficulty level $n$ is chosen randomly and uniformly from $1$ to $120,$ and then a puzzle piece with a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the puzzle piece selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the room difficulty level, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $120.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the puzzle piece number, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,120\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{120+1}{2}=60.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (60.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 61.5 \\\\  & =30.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208563 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "753c2aae-9fc7-5b5b-930e-87222f30f7a2" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64094c0c-e4c0-599f-b265-b424a2b28840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64094c0c-e4c0-599f-b265-b424a2b28840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531084} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b3cc385-06d8-50b5-ae5b-ce22d4c1e608" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5923cf06-a8dd-57e8-81ff-5f4a640b9a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-275578" :question/problem "A robot starts at the bottom of a staircase with $n$ steps, where $n$ is an integer chosen uniformly at random from $1$ to $10.$ The robot then climbs a uniformly random number of steps between $1$ and $n.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of steps the robot climbs?\n\nThe expected number of steps the robot climbs is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "This is a two-step random process: first, an integer $n$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ through $10,$ and then a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ through $n.$\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number: the number of steps in the staircase. Since an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $10,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,10\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the number of steps the robot climbs, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,10\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{10+1}{2}=5.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6.5 \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208563 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "753c2aae-9fc7-5b5b-930e-87222f30f7a2" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Tower Property in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc71da5-b68e-5e22-b93f-45f1f89b8b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cc71da5-b68e-5e22-b93f-45f1f89b8b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531200} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05f3dbe-00ff-5d9e-ab0d-54ff37d66045" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d15a80b9-3b38-5de0-953c-45a827aaaf2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-275580" :question/problem "A robotic arm randomly selects a box in a warehouse containing $n$ items from $1$ to $150,$ with each value equally likely. The arm then randomly picks one of these $n$ items and scans its position number (from $1$ to $n,$ each value equally likely).\n\nWhat is the expected position number scanned?\n\nThe expected position number scanned is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "This is a two-step random process: first, a box is selected containing $n$ items uniformly from $1$ through $150,$ and then an item is chosen uniformly in one of the positions numbered $1$ through $n.$\n\n- Let $Y$ be the number of items in the box. Since this is an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $150,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,150\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the position number scanned, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,150\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{150+1}{2}=75.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (75.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 76.5 \\\\  & =38.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
